You're trying to figure out why your Firebase database is getting slammed with reads. That's a great question. Instrumenting your Firebase calls can help you identify the source of the issue. Here's a step-by-step guide to get you started:

First, you'll need to set up Firebase DebugView. It's a tool that allows you to see the network requests made by your app, including Firebase calls. To do this, you'll need to:

1. Open your Firebase console and navigate to the "DebugView" tab.
2. Click on the "New DebugView" button and select the Firebase project you want to monitor.
3. You'll get a unique URL that you'll need to add to your app. This will allow DebugView to capture the network requests made by your app.

Next, you'll need to add some code to your app to include the DebugView URL in your Firebase calls. This will help you see the specific requests being made to your database. You can do this by adding the following code to your app:

```javascript
import { getAnalytics } from 'firebase/analytics';

const analytics = getAnalytics(app);
const debugViewUrl = 'YOUR_DEBUGVIEW_URL';
const debugView = new DebugView(debugViewUrl);

// When making a Firebase call, include the debugView object
firebase.database().ref('your/path').once('value').then((snapshot) => {
  debugView.log('Read from database');
  // ...
});
```

Now, when you make a Firebase call, you should see the request appear in the DebugView. This will help you identify which parts of your app are making the most reads.

Another way to instrument your Firebase calls is to use the Firebase Performance Monitoring feature. This will give you more detailed information about the performance of your Firebase calls, including the number of reads, writes, and deletes.

To set up Firebase Performance Monitoring, follow these steps:

1. Open your Firebase console and navigate to the "Performance" tab.
2. Click on the "New Performance Monitoring" button and select the Firebase project you want to monitor.
3. You'll get a unique API key that you'll need to add to your app.

Then, you can use the Firebase Performance Monitoring SDK to track the performance of your Firebase calls. Here's an example of how you might use it:

```javascript
import { initializeApp } from 'firebase/app';
import { getPerformance } from 'firebase/performance';

const app = initializeApp();
const performance = getPerformance(app);

// When making a Firebase call, include the performance object
firebase.database().ref('your/path').once('value').then((snapshot) => {
  performance.measure('Database read', 'start', 'end');
  // ...
});
```

By using DebugView and Firebase Performance Monitoring, you should be able to identify which parts of your app are making the most reads and optimize your database usage accordingly.
